      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your stress, and I apologize for the earlier distraction. While I cannot actually delete the goblins from my internal processing, I can certainly focus strictly on the legal requirements for your audit without the metaphors.\n\nRegarding the retention period for customer email addresses and shipping information under GDPR (specifically in the context of a French business in Lyon), there is no single universal \"delete after X days\" rule that applies to all data. Instead, the retention period is determined by two main factors:\n\n1.  **Statutory Retention Periods**: You must keep records for as long as required by French or EU law for tax, accounting, and commercial purposes. For example, French tax law often requires keeping accounting documents and invoices for at least **10 years**. Shipping records and order histories often fall under this commercial record-keeping requirement.\n2.  **Legitimate Interest vs. Consent**: If you are keeping the data for the purpose of fulfilling a contract (the sale) or for legitimate business interests (like fraud prevention), you generally cannot delete it until the legal obligation to keep it expires.\n\n**Crucial Step for the Audit:**\nYou must conduct a **Data Retention Review** (often called a \"",
